course: dictionary
lesson: 15198
title: Seismic: shaking earth and big change
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Seismic is a word about shaking. It means to do with earthquakes or the shaking of the Earth. «The seismic waves reached the town within minutes of the quake.» Waves are moving energy, and a quake is an earthquake, so seismic is about the ground moving.
explain: Seismic can also mean very big and important, like a change that shakes everything. «The new rule caused a seismic shift in how the whole school works.» Nobody felt the ground move here. The clue is shift, which means a change, and the size of it shows it is huge.
explain: To pick the right meaning, read the words around seismic. Ask: is this sentence about the ground, or about something huge changing? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: Seismic means to do with earthquakes or the shaking of the Earth.
fact: «Seismic waves» are waves of energy that travel through the Earth after an earthquake.
fact: A seismometer, also called a seismograph, records seismic waves.
fact: New Zealand, Japan and California have a lot of seismic activity because they sit on the edges of Earth's plates.
fact: «A seismic shift» means a very big, important change.
exercise: «The seismic waves reached the town within minutes.» What is seismic about here? | A kind of sea animal | * The shaking of the ground | Bright sunshine on the water | A very quiet sound
exercise: «A seismometer records the shaking of the Earth.» What does a seismometer do? | It makes earthquakes happen | It keeps the wind away from houses | * It records seismic waves | It measures the temperature of the sea
exercise: «The new rule was a seismic shift for the whole school.» What does seismic mean here? | A tired and slow walk | A small hole in the floor | * A very big, important change | A loud and angry noise
test: Why do New Zealand, Japan and California have a lot of seismic activity? | They are the hottest places on Earth | They are all on flat deserts | * They sit on the edges of Earth's plates | They have the most rain in the world
test: «In a seismic zone, buildings are made to sway.» Why? | So they look taller from far away | So they can float on water | * So they do not fall when the ground shakes | So the wind cannot see them
exam: Which sentence uses seismic to mean a huge, important change? | «The seismic zone was covered in snow.» | «The seismometer sat quietly on the table.» | * «The vote brought a seismic shift in how the club is run.» | «The seismic waves shook the old bridge.»
